# Cloudflare Pages redirects for the public site.
#
# THIS FILE DEFINES ONE RULE. It exists so the next person looks here,
# reads why, and does not spend an afternoon rediscovering it.
#
# THE www -> APEX REDIRECT CANNOT BE EXPRESSED HERE. Pages matches the source column on PATH ONLY.
# Cloudflare's own `_redirects` documentation lists "Domain-level redirects" as unsupported and
# gives `workers.example.com/* workers.example.com/blog/:splat 301` as an example of a line that
# does not work. Invalid lines are ignored rather than reported, so a hostname rule written here
# would look correct in review, deploy without error, and silently do nothing -- which is worse
# than not writing it. That much is still true of THIS file.
#
# THE HOSTNAME RULE NOW LIVES IN `functions/_middleware.js`, which can read `Host` because it is a
# Function rather than a static rule table. It redirects every non-canonical hostname — including
# `www.rwally.com` AND the `*.rwally.pages.dev` hostnames — to `rwally.com` with a 301. That is not
# only a www->apex tidy-up: the SAME FUNCTION also enforces the sanctions-jurisdiction geofence
# (card 212) directly, in code, on every one of those hostnames — it is no longer solely a WAF rule
# scoped to the `rwally.com` zone, which is what left the pages.dev hostnames as an unfiltered path
# before this. Read that file before changing anything here. A zone-level Bulk Redirect in the
# dashboard remains a valid alternative for the www leg, but it would not cover pages.dev, and it
# would not carry the geofence either.
#
# NO CANONICALIZATION RULE IS NEEDED EITHER. Pages already redirects HTML pages to their
# extension-less counterparts: `/faq.html` to `/faq`, and `/index.html` to `/`. Those are the
# canonical URLs, and they are what sitemap.xml lists. Restating that behaviour as a rule here
# would add a line to maintain that changes nothing.
#
# IF A RULE IS EVER ADDED: one per line, `from  to  status`, source is a path, and the file is
# capped at 2,000 static and 100 dynamic redirects. Put the reason for the rule in a comment above
# it -- a redirect nobody can explain is a redirect nobody can safely delete.

# THE ONE RULE. `/risks` was a page of this site from launch until 2026-09-05, when the owner
# consolidated every warning, limit and unresolved question onto a single page and `risks.html` was
# retired into `disclaimers.html`. The old URL is in `sitemap.xml` history, in the repository, and in
# whatever anyone has bookmarked or linked, and the page it pointed at is the one a reader most needs
# to reach. A 301 rather than a 302 because the move is permanent: the page is not coming back.
#
# This is a PATH rule, which is the kind `_redirects` actually supports, so unlike the hostname rule
# above it works here. Pages serves the extension-less form, so `/risks` is the URL to match;
# `/risks.html` redirects to `/risks` first and lands on this rule on the second hop.

/risks  /disclaimers  301

# THE SIX RULES BELOW. The nine-page site collapsed on 2026-09-05 to one scroll page plus a
# Disclaimers page. Every one of the retired URLs is still in sitemap.xml's git history, in the
# repository at large, and in whatever anyone has bookmarked or linked from elsewhere, so each gets
# a 301 rather than a 302: none of these pages is coming back.
#
# ONE OF THE SIX SPLITS FROM THE OTHER FIVE. `/status` redirects to `/disclaimers`, not to `/`,
# because the status page carried the deployment record and the claims register, and that material
# survived the collapse onto the Disclaimers page rather than onto the homepage. The other five,
# agents, who-its-for, operators, faq and vision, were narrative pages explaining the protocol, and
# that narrative now lives on the homepage, so all five redirect there.
#
# Pages serves the extension-less form, the same as the `/risks` rule above, so `/operators.html`
# redirects to `/operators` first and lands on these rules on the second hop.
#
# `/how-it-works` WAS THE SEVENTH RULE, AND IT CAME BACK AS A REAL PAGE. The site was rebuilt to five
# pages after the collapse, and one of them is `how-it-works.html` again — in `src/pages.ts`, in the
# nav, and behind the homepage's "How it works" button. The rule stayed, so Pages 301'd the page to
# `/` before it could be served: the nav link bounced readers to the homepage and the page was
# unreachable in production, while every build, test and gate stayed green, because none of them
# serve through this file. Removed 2026-09-23.
#
# THE CHECK THAT WOULD HAVE CAUGHT IT: a redirect SOURCE that is also a live page in `src/pages.ts`
# shadows that page completely. Before adding any rule here, make sure its source is not a PageId.

/agents         /              301
/who-its-for    /              301
/operators      /              301
/faq            /              301
/vision         /              301
/status         /disclaimers   301
